            <div xml:id="d657">

                <pb facs="0925" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">341.1154L58/257: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-04-21T12:00:00+00:00">April 21,
                            1934—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 21—9:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>186. Department’s 152, April 17, 6 p.m.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d657-f1"/> I called
                    on Sir John Simon last evening by appointment to discuss orally again the Lewis
                    gun case <pb facs="0926" n="826" xml:id="pg826"/> with him as set forth in the
                    Department’s instruction 255, February 20th. He told me a written note was in
                    process of completion, but for my information the position of the British
                    Government remained unchanged in this matter of a Belgian corporation. In brief
                    he gave me to understand that after a careful review of the case upon receipt of
                    the last American note (see my 548 of March 6) the British Government’s position
                    was unaltered and would not consider reopening the matter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
